# The Museum's candidates: classic synth sounds rebuilt from their published settings
# (decisions D88, D103, D104). A sound moves from here into resources/presets/12-museum.csbank,
# and so into the plugin, when the user admits it in a side-by-side on the audition page.
# This folder is not built into the plugin.
#
# DX7 E.PIANO 1 was admitted on 13 November 2026 (decision D107), DX7 BASS 1 (D111), Prophet-5 Sync II and
# Brass (D115) and Minimoog Fat Bass (D118) on 14 November.

[Minimoog Trilogy Lead]
room = Museum
# "Emerson, Lake and Palmer's Trilogy", from the same Sound Charts page. The chart: oscillator 2
# at 2' and level 8 carries the tune; oscillators 1 and 3 at 16', three octaves under it, at
# level 2; all three on the square wave; filter cutoff -1, emphasis 6, amount of contour 3, both
# keyboard-control switches on; filter contour attack 0, decay 800 msec, sustain 0; loudness
# contour attack 0, decay 400 msec, sustain 10; glide at 5, decay switch on. One voice, the
# lowest key sounds; a struck note starts the filter's sweep again, a joined one does not.
# From the chart: ranges, levels, switches. Fitted to a recording of a real Model D set to this
# chart, in which the player strikes every note: the pulse width (that instrument's "square"
# is nearer a third than a half: its second harmonic is strong and its third nearly gone), the
# cutoff (2.7 kHz at middle C; the dial reading alone would give 1.4 kHz), emphasis 6 (0.4),
# the contour's reach (1.7 octaves) and time. No glide: the chart has it on at 5, but in the
# recording each new pitch is there within a hundredth of a second, and a glide of even 25 ms
# made the notes' starts sound smoothed to the user.
# Two things at a note's edges are the instrument's, not the chart's. The filter's sweep cannot
# start at once: with the dial at 0 its brightness still takes about a hundredth of a second to
# come up (the manual gives 10 ms as the shortest attack), which the user heard as "more of a
# ramp up curve" where this preset opened in a thousandth. And a note dies in under a tenth of
# a second when the key goes up, four times faster than the chart's decay time read as a
# release would give.
g1 = VA shape=pulse pw=0.3 oct=-1 level=0.15
g2 = VA shape=pulse pw=0.3 oct=2 level=0.8
g3 = VA shape=pulse pw=0.3 oct=-1 fine=3 level=0.15
p1 = Ladder cutoff=2748 res=0.405 key=1 drive=6 bass=0
env1 = attack=2 decay=1140 sustain=1 release=300 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 velocity=0
env2 = attack=10 decay=3850 sustain=0 release=3850 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1
voice = env2ToP1=0.17 velToP1=0 drift=1
play = mode=legato priority=low
master = 0

[TX81Z LatelyBass]
room = Museum
# The Yamaha TX81Z's LatelyBass (1987, preset C15): the bass of late-eighties house and new jack
# swing. Play it around the octave under middle C: it sounds two octaves under the key, as on the
# instrument (the voice is transposed an octave down and its carrier runs at half the note).
# The voice, from the instrument's own preset table (read out of its firmware): algorithm 2, two
# modulators into one into the carrier. The carrier and the modulator on it run at the same
# pitch (1.7 radians at a hard key, fading with the note): the round, hollow body. On that
# modulator sit two more an octave higher, both gone within a third of a second: a sine with
# full feedback (3 radians at a hard key: the bark), and one on the TX81Z's fifth wave, a whole
# sine squeezed into half the cycle (1.3 radians: the click). A light key leaves most of the
# bark out; the note itself is as loud at any velocity, as on the instrument. A slow wobble of
# pitch, five cents either way, is the voice's own LFO.
# Nothing is fitted: levels, ratios, rates, rate scaling and the velocity law (the firmware's
# table) are the chip's rules converted; the feedback follows operator 4's level, as on the
# chip. Against a recording of a real TX81Z playing this voice (one note, hardest velocity) the
# first sixteen harmonics are within 0.9 dB on average over half a second, and against the
# chip's rules within 0.3 to 1.4 dB over fifteen keys and velocities.
# Needed the operator waves on the FM4 generator (decision D117).
g1 = FM4 algo=twoIntoOne ratio2=1 ratio3=2 ratio4=2 level2=0 level3=0 level4=0 fb=0 wave3=W5 oct=-2 level=0.8
p1 = Off
env1 = attack=1 decay=4700 sustain=0 release=294 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 velocity=0 keyTime=0.25
env2 = attack=1 decay=294 sustain=0 release=294 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 keyTime=0.25
env3 = attack=1 decay=511 sustain=0.0158 release=363 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 keyTime=0.125
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level2 amount=0.1294 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level2 amount=0.3362 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env2 dst=g1.level3 amount=0.1137 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env2 dst=g1.level3 amount=0.2954 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.level4 amount=0.4315 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.level4 amount=0.2025 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.fb amount=1.0000 via=velocity
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.fb amount=0.0877 via=velocity
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.fb amount=-0.0616
lfo1 = shape=triangle rate=3.5 retrigger=off
mod = src=lfo1 dst=pitch amount=0.00115
voice = env2ToP1=0 velToP1=0
play = mode=poly voices=8
master = 0
